#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:51
msgid "Checks in a ticket, timeslot ticket or pass. One nano id namespace covers all three - the endpoint works out which type the scanned code belongs to."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:52
#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:63
#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:74
msgid "Scanner role or manage_woocommerce"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:54
msgid "Checked in. Body carries sMessage, the ticket/pass row as ticket, valid_from, valid_to, max_uses (tickets and timeslot tickets), sPhotoURL (passes) and sHexColor."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:55
msgid "Refused for a reason the door staff needs to read: outside its validity window, on cooldown after a previous scan (iCooldown, iCooldownOver), already at its maximum uses, or being scanned at another door at the same moment. Body carries sMessage and sHexColor."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:56
msgid "No valid credentials, or no ticket, timeslot ticket or pass matches the id."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:57
#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:68
msgid "The nano id in the path is not shaped like one, so nothing was looked up."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:62
msgid "Checks in a guest pass - a pass issued under a parent pass. Same responses as the endpoint above, plus sPhotoURL on success when the guest has a profile photo."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:65
msgid "Checked in. Body carries sMessage, sPhotoURL and sHexColor."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:66
msgid "Outside its validity window, on cooldown, at its maximum uses, or being scanned at another door at the same moment."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:67
msgid "No valid credentials, or the guest pass (or its parent pass) could not be found."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:73
msgid "The 50 most recent check-ins performed by the authenticated scanner account, newest first. Use it to fill a history list in your app. Each entry has sTypeKey (ticket, timeslot, pass or guestpass), sHolderName and sCreatedUTC, an ISO-8601 timestamp carrying the site timezone offset."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:76
msgid "Body carries aHistory, an array of entries. An account that has scanned nothing gets an empty array."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:77
msgid "No valid credentials."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:82
msgid "Returns a timeslot ticket as a calendar file. This one is deliberately open: the nano id in the URL is itself the secret, and the confirmation email hands it to the customer so they can add their booking to a calendar on any device."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:83
msgid "None - the nano id is the secret"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:85
msgid "A text/calendar file, not JSON."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:86
msgid "No timeslot ticket matches the id."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:96
msgid "This is the check-in API external scanner apps use to validate tickets, timeslot tickets and passes."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:97
msgid "Turn the API and the built-in scanner page on or off below, and set the colors returned to the scanner for each scan result."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:102
msgid "A note on the built-in scanner"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:103
msgid "The check-in page that ships with this plugin does the job: it opens the camera in a browser, decodes the QR code and checks the visitor in. For a small door, or a venue scanning a few hundred people a night, it is all you need."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:104
msgid "It is not the optimal tool, though. A browser page cannot drive a phone the way a native app can: focus and decoding are slower, every single scan needs a live connection, and a locked screen or a switched tab interrupts the camera. If you scan at volume, or at a gate where the signal is poor, a standalone native scanner app will be faster and far more reliable."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:105
msgid "That is what the API is for. Turn it on and any app - one your developer builds, or an existing scanner app that can call a URL - can check tickets, timeslot tickets and passes in against this site. The endpoints are documented below: plain authenticated GET requests, so a working prototype is an afternoon of work rather than a project."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: link to the plugin support forum.
#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:109
#, php-format
msgid "If you would rather not build it yourself, get in touch on %s and we can see if we can figure something out."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:110
msgid "the plugin support forum"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:117
msgid "Enable API"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:117
msgid "An API is how an external app - like a scanner app on your door staff's phones - talks to this site to check tickets in and out. Turn this on only if something outside this site needs to call the check-in endpoints; the built-in scanner below works without it."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:128
msgid "API endpoint reference"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:130
msgid "Base URL"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:132
msgid "Every path below is relative to that. All of them are GET requests and return JSON, apart from the calendar file."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:134
msgid "Authentication"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:135
msgid "HTTP Basic authentication with a WordPress account on this site that holds the Scanner role, or any account that can manage WooCommerce. Create one account per device so the check-in history tells you which door did what."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:136
msgid "Use an Application Password rather than the real account password - WordPress generates them under Users, they only work for API requests and can be revoked one at a time. Send requests over HTTPS: Basic authentication puts the credentials in a header on every single request."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:137
msgid "Outside callers are only accepted while Enable API is on above. The built-in scanner page authenticates with its own login session instead, so it keeps working either way."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:139
msgid "Endpoints"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:144
msgid "Requires:"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:153
msgid "Result colors"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:154
msgid "Every JSON response carries sHexColor, the color configured above for that outcome. Painting your result screen with it means the colors your staff learn to read are set here, in wp-admin, and not baked into the app."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:156
msgid "Example"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:158
msgid "A nano id is 21 characters of A-Z, a-z and 0-9. The QR code itself contains the full check-in URL for this site (…/scanner/checkin/<nano id>, with /guest appended for a guest pass), so your app can either request that URL directly or take the last path segment and build the request against this site's REST base."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:164
msgid "Enable built-in scanner"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:164
msgid "Turns on the check-in page at /check-in/ - a full-screen camera scanner your door staff open on a phone, with no wp-admin around it. They sign in with a WordPress account that has the Scanner role, and a link to it appears in this plugin's menu. Leave it off if you check people in with a separate scanner app instead. This toggle is independent of the API above."
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:176
msgid "Successful Check-in (200)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/api/setting-pages/api-page-content.php:176
msgid "Color the scanner app should show when a ticket, timeslot ticket or pass is successfully checked in."
msgstr ""

